discuss realistic trajectory, identify when nursing home placement may become necessary (wandering, incontinence, behavioral disturbances). Early planning reduces crisis decision-making and caregiver burden. The geriatrician should initiate these discussions at the time of diagnosis, not after a crisis. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 73; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male named Earl has just been diagnosed with mild Alzheimer''s disease. His nephew asks the clinician, ''What should we plan for over the next few years?'' Which of the following represents the most appropriate and comprehensive response regarding the caregiver trajectory in Alzheimer''s disease?', '["Focus exclusively on pharmacological management of the patient''s symptoms and defer all social planning discussions to a separate social work appointment next year", "Provide anticipatory guidance: caregiving demands will progressively increase as the disease advances through mild, moderate, and severe stages; early-stage planning should include legal/financial preparations (power of attorney, advance directives), identifying future care resources, caregiver support enrollment, and discussing the likely eventual need for residential care", "Advise the family to purchase a long-term care insurance policy immediately, as this is the only reliable funding source for future dementia care", "Reassure the caregiver that Alzheimer''s disease has a very gradual course requiring minimal caregiving adjustments over time, and no long-term planning is necessary at this early stage", "Tell the nephew that home care for Alzheimer''s is unsustainable beyond 6 months and immediate nursing home placement is the clinically recommended option"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Anticipatory guidance in dementia caregiving** is a proactive, staged planning approach recognizing that Alzheimer''s disease progresses through predictable stages with escalating care demands. Early-stage planning (FAST Stage 3–4) should address: (1) **Legal**: durable power of attorney (financial and healthcare), advance directives (POLST, living will); (2) **Financial**: benefits counseling, long-term care insurance eligibility, spend-down planning; (3) **Social/community**: enroll caregiver in support groups, identify adult day programs, respite care resources; (4) **Future care planning**: discuss realistic trajectory, identify when nursing home placement may become necessary (wandering, incontinence, behavioral disturbances). Early planning reduces crisis decision-making and caregiver burden. The geriatrician should initiate these discussions at the time of diagnosis, not after a crisis. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 73; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old female named Lillian with advanced dementia is cared for exclusively by family members in a multigenerational household. The family is from a cultural background in which nursing home placement is considered deeply stigmatizing and a betrayal of family duty. Despite the patient''s increasing care needs, the family categorically refuses institutional care and is struggling significantly. Which of the following represents the most culturally competent clinical approach?', '["Arrange nursing home placement without the family''s consent as the clinician knows what is medically best, regardless of patient and family values", "Discharge the patient from geriatric care and refer exclusively to a social worker, as cultural issues are outside the scope of geriatric medicine", "Respect the family''s cultural values while honestly discussing the increasing care demands; collaboratively explore culturally acceptable alternatives such as in-home skilled nursing, PACE, culturally aligned adult day programs, and offer caregiver burnout resources without imposing institutional placement", "Inform the family that their cultural beliefs are clinically inappropriate and that nursing home placement is legally mandated once a patient requires assistance with more than three ADLs", "File a report with Adult Protective Services for medical neglect because the family is refusing institutional care"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Cultural competency in geriatric caregiving** is an essential clinical skill. Research shows that Hispanic, Asian-American, and African-American families are significantly more likely to provide home-based care for dementia patients and are less likely to use nursing home placement due to cultural values of **filial piety** and family obligation. The clinician''s role is NOT to override cultural values but to: (1) **Acknowledge** and **respect** the family''s values; (2) **Honestly assess** escalating care needs; (3) **Offer culturally acceptable alternatives**: in-home aides, PACE, culturally aligned day programs, community health workers from the same cultural background; (4) **Provide resources** in the family''s language; (5) **Monitor for caregiver burnout** and patient safety — without judgment. Forcing institutional placement against family values is ethically and legally inappropriate when the patient is safe. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female named Phyllis with advanced dementia is cared for exclusively by family members in a multigenerational household. The family is from a cultural background in which nursing home placement is considered deeply stigmatizing and a betrayal of family duty. Despite the patient''s increasing care needs, the family categorically refuses institutional care and is struggling significantly. Which of the following represents the most culturally competent clinical approach?', '["Inform the family that their cultural beliefs are clinically inappropriate and that nursing home placement is legally mandated once a patient requires assistance with more than three ADLs", "File a report with Adult Protective Services for medical neglect because the family is refusing institutional care", "Discharge the patient from geriatric care and refer exclusively to a social worker, as cultural issues are outside the scope of geriatric medicine", "Respect the family''s cultural values while honestly discussing the increasing care demands; collaboratively explore culturally acceptable alternatives such as in-home skilled nursing, PACE, culturally aligned adult day programs, and offer caregiver burnout resources without imposing institutional placement", "Arrange nursing home placement without the family''s consent as the clinician knows what is medically best, regardless of patient and family values"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Cultural competency in geriatric caregiving** is an essential clinical skill. Research shows that Hispanic, Asian-American, and African-American families are significantly more likely to provide home-based care for dementia patients and are less likely to use nursing home placement due to cultural values of **filial piety** and family obligation. The clinician''s role is NOT to override cultural values but to: (1) **Acknowledge** and **respect** the family''s values; (2) **Honestly assess** escalating care needs; (3) **Offer culturally acceptable alternatives**: in-home aides, PACE, culturally aligned day programs, community health workers from the same cultural background; (4) **Provide resources** in the family''s language; (5) **Monitor for caregiver burnout** and patient safety — without judgment. Forcing institutional placement against family values is ethically and legally inappropriate when the patient is safe. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named Henry with advanced dementia is cared for exclusively by family members in a multigenerational household. The family is from a cultural background in which nursing home placement is considered deeply stigmatizing and a betrayal of family duty. Despite the patient''s increasing care needs, the family categorically refuses institutional care and is struggling significantly. Which of the following represents the most culturally competent clinical approach?', '["Respect the family''s cultural values while honestly discussing the increasing care demands; collaboratively explore culturally acceptable alternatives such as in-home skilled nursing, PACE, culturally aligned adult day programs, and offer caregiver burnout resources without imposing institutional placement", "Arrange nursing home placement without the family''s consent as the clinician knows what is medically best, regardless of patient and family values", "Inform the family that their cultural beliefs are clinically inappropriate and that nursing home placement is legally mandated once a patient requires assistance with more than three ADLs", "Discharge the patient from geriatric care and refer exclusively to a social worker, as cultural issues are outside the scope of geriatric medicine", "File a report with Adult Protective Services for medical neglect because the family is refusing institutional care"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Cultural competency in geriatric caregiving** is an essential clinical skill. Research shows that Hispanic, Asian-American, and African-American families are significantly more likely to provide home-based care for dementia patients and are less likely to use nursing home placement due to cultural values of **filial piety** and family obligation. The clinician''s role is NOT to override cultural values but to: (1) **Acknowledge** and **respect** the family''s values; (2) **Honestly assess** escalating care needs; (3) **Offer culturally acceptable alternatives**: in-home aides, PACE, culturally aligned day programs, community health workers from the same cultural background; (4) **Provide resources** in the family''s language; (5) **Monitor for caregiver burnout** and patient safety — without judgment. Forcing institutional placement against family values is ethically and legally inappropriate when the patient is safe. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
