    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female named Elizabeth with advanced dementia is cared for exclusively by family members in a multigenerational household. The family is from a cultural background in which nursing home placement is considered deeply stigmatizing and a betrayal of family duty. Despite the patient''s increasing care needs, the family categorically refuses institutional care and is struggling significantly. Which of the following represents the most culturally competent clinical approach?', '["Respect the family''s cultural values while honestly discussing the increasing care demands; collaboratively explore culturally acceptable alternatives such as in-home skilled nursing, PACE, culturally aligned adult day programs, and offer caregiver burnout resources without imposing institutional placement", "File a report with Adult Protective Services for medical neglect because the family is refusing institutional care", "Discharge the patient from geriatric care and refer exclusively to a social worker, as cultural issues are outside the scope of geriatric medicine", "Inform the family that their cultural beliefs are clinically inappropriate and that nursing home placement is legally mandated once a patient requires assistance with more than three ADLs", "Arrange nursing home placement without the family''s consent as the clinician knows what is medically best, regardless of patient and family values"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Cultural competency in geriatric caregiving** is an essential clinical skill. Research shows that Hispanic, Asian-American, and African-American families are significantly more likely to provide home-based care for dementia patients and are less likely to use nursing home placement due to cultural values of **filial piety** and family obligation. The clinician''s role is NOT to override cultural values but to: (1) **Acknowledge** and **respect** the family''s values; (2) **Honestly assess** escalating care needs; (3) **Offer culturally acceptable alternatives**: in-home aides, PACE, culturally aligned day programs, community health workers from the same cultural background; (4) **Provide resources** in the family''s language; (5) **Monitor for caregiver burnout** and patient safety — without judgment. Forcing institutional placement against family values is ethically and legally inappropriate when the patient is safe. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female named Rose with advanced dementia is cared for exclusively by family members in a multigenerational household. The family is from a cultural background in which nursing home placement is considered deeply stigmatizing and a betrayal of family duty. Despite the patient''s increasing care needs, the family categorically refuses institutional care and is struggling significantly. Which of the following represents the most culturally competent clinical approach?', '["Arrange nursing home placement without the family''s consent as the clinician knows what is medically best, regardless of patient and family values", "Respect the family''s cultural values while honestly discussing the increasing care demands; collaboratively explore culturally acceptable alternatives such as in-home skilled nursing, PACE, culturally aligned adult day programs, and offer caregiver burnout resources without imposing institutional placement", "Inform the family that their cultural beliefs are clinically inappropriate and that nursing home placement is legally mandated once a patient requires assistance with more than three ADLs", "File a report with Adult Protective Services for medical neglect because the family is refusing institutional care", "Discharge the patient from geriatric care and refer exclusively to a social worker, as cultural issues are outside the scope of geriatric medicine"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Cultural competency in geriatric caregiving** is an essential clinical skill. Research shows that Hispanic, Asian-American, and African-American families are significantly more likely to provide home-based care for dementia patients and are less likely to use nursing home placement due to cultural values of **filial piety** and family obligation. The clinician''s role is NOT to override cultural values but to: (1) **Acknowledge** and **respect** the family''s values; (2) **Honestly assess** escalating care needs; (3) **Offer culturally acceptable alternatives**: in-home aides, PACE, culturally aligned day programs, community health workers from the same cultural background; (4) **Provide resources** in the family''s language; (5) **Monitor for caregiver burnout** and patient safety — without judgment. Forcing institutional placement against family values is ethically and legally inappropriate when the patient is safe. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
