    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Elizabeth with mild cognitive impairment and a recent fall is being discharged home. A home safety evaluation is ordered. Which of the following home modifications has the strongest evidence for reducing falls in community-dwelling older adults?', '["Placing the patient in physical restraints in a recliner chair during all waking hours to prevent unassisted ambulation", "Moving the patient to the ground floor only while installing wall-to-wall padded flooring throughout the entire home", "Installation of grab bars in the bathroom (tub, shower, toilet), removal of loose throw rugs, adequate night lighting in hallways and bathrooms, securing loose electrical cords, and referral to occupational therapy for a formal home safety assessment", "Purchasing an expensive commercial smart-home fall detection system as the primary fall prevention strategy, without addressing structural home hazards", "Prescribing a wheelchair for all indoor ambulation to eliminate the risk of falls from walking"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Home safety modifications** are a cornerstone of fall prevention in community-dwelling older adults. The strongest evidence-based structural modifications include: (1) **Bathroom**: grab bars (toilet, tub, shower), non-slip bath mats, raised toilet seat; (2) **Floor surfaces**: remove loose throw rugs (major fall hazard), secure carpets, ensure level thresholds; (3) **Lighting**: adequate night lighting (night lights, motion-activated lights), particularly in hallways and bathrooms; (4) **Electrical and clutter**: remove trailing cords, clear pathways; (5) **Stairs**: secure handrails on both sides, contrasting step edges. A formal **occupational therapy (OT) home assessment** is the gold standard — OTs identify individualized hazards and recommend targeted modifications. The CAPABLE (Community Aging in Place—Advancing Better Living for Elders) model combining OT, nursing, and home repairs significantly reduces falls and disability. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 41; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
