ility. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 41; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old male named Clarence with heart failure is seen in the clinic in late July. He lives alone in a third-floor apartment with no air conditioning. His core body temperature is 38.8°C, he is mildly confused, and his medications include a loop diuretic and a beta-blocker. He income is $720/month fixed income. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate and coordinated clinical response?', '["Admit to the hospital solely for the purpose of providing a cool environment and discharge after 24 hours without addressing the underlying housing hazard", "Discharge the patient home immediately with strict instructions to open windows and drink 8 glasses of water daily, as this is sufficient to prevent hyperthermia", "Prescribe a higher dose of loop diuretics to counteract the fluid shift caused by heat exposure and plan routine follow-up in 1 month", "Tell the patient that air conditioning is a luxury item and their housing arrangement requires no medical intervention", "Address the immediate heat-related illness medically; provide cooling measures and IV hydration if indicated; connect the patient with LIHEAP (Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program) for air conditioning unit funding; explore emergency housing options or ''cooling center'' access; assess medication safety in the heat context"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Extreme temperature exposure** is a critical **housing-related social determinant of health** in older adults. Physiological reasons for heat vulnerability: (1) impaired thermoregulatory response; (2) reduced thirst sensation (dehydration risk); (3) medications (diuretics promote dehydration; beta-blockers impair cardiovascular adaptation; anticholinergics impair sweating). **LIHEAP** (Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program) provides federally funded energy bill assistance and in some states emergency air conditioning units. Clinical response: (1) treat heat illness; (2) review and adjust heat-risk medications; (3) connect with LIHEAP; (4) refer to **cooling centers** (community shelters with AC open during heat waves); (5) consider home visiting programs. Heat waves are associated with significant excess mortality in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named Charles with heart failure is seen in the clinic in late July. He lives alone in a third-floor apartment with no air conditioning. His core body temperature is 38.8°C, he is mildly confused, and his medications include a loop diuretic and a beta-blocker. He income is $880/month Social Security Disability. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate and coordinated clinical response?', '["Prescribe a higher dose of loop diuretics to counteract the fluid shift caused by heat exposure and plan routine follow-up in 1 month", "Discharge the patient home immediately with strict instructions to open windows and drink 8 glasses of water daily, as this is sufficient to prevent hyperthermia", "Tell the patient that air conditioning is a luxury item and their housing arrangement requires no medical intervention", "Admit to the hospital solely for the purpose of providing a cool environment and discharge after 24 hours without addressing the underlying housing hazard", "Address the immediate heat-related illness medically; provide cooling measures and IV hydration if indicated; connect the patient with LIHEAP (Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program) for air conditioning unit funding; explore emergency housing options or ''cooling center'' access; assess medication safety in the heat context"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Extreme temperature exposure** is a critical **housing-related social determinant of health** in older adults. Physiological reasons for heat vulnerability: (1) impaired thermoregulatory response; (2) reduced thirst sensation (dehydration risk); (3) medications (diuretics promote dehydration; beta-blockers impair cardiovascular adaptation; anticholinergics impair sweating). **LIHEAP** (Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program) provides federally funded energy bill assistance and in some states emergency air conditioning units. Clinical response: (1) treat heat illness; (2) review and adjust heat-risk medications; (3) connect with LIHEAP; (4) refer to **cooling centers** (community shelters with AC open during heat waves); (5) consider home visiting programs. Heat waves are associated with significant excess mortality in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
