    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old female named Florence with heart failure is seen in the clinic in late July. She lives alone in a third-floor apartment with no air conditioning. Her core body temperature is 38.8°C, she is mildly confused, and her medications include a loop diuretic and a beta-blocker. She income is $1,050/month pension. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate and coordinated clinical response?', '["Address the immediate heat-related illness medically; provide cooling measures and IV hydration if indicated; connect the patient with LIHEAP (Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program) for air conditioning unit funding; explore emergency housing options or ''cooling center'' access; assess medication safety in the heat context", "Discharge the patient home immediately with strict instructions to open windows and drink 8 glasses of water daily, as this is sufficient to prevent hyperthermia", "Prescribe a higher dose of loop diuretics to counteract the fluid shift caused by heat exposure and plan routine follow-up in 1 month", "Admit to the hospital solely for the purpose of providing a cool environment and discharge after 24 hours without addressing the underlying housing hazard", "Tell the patient that air conditioning is a luxury item and their housing arrangement requires no medical intervention"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Extreme temperature exposure** is a critical **housing-related social determinant of health** in older adults. Physiological reasons for heat vulnerability: (1) impaired thermoregulatory response; (2) reduced thirst sensation (dehydration risk); (3) medications (diuretics promote dehydration; beta-blockers impair cardiovascular adaptation; anticholinergics impair sweating). **LIHEAP** (Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program) provides federally funded energy bill assistance and in some states emergency air conditioning units. Clinical response: (1) treat heat illness; (2) review and adjust heat-risk medications; (3) connect with LIHEAP; (4) refer to **cooling centers** (community shelters with AC open during heat waves); (5) consider home visiting programs. Heat waves are associated with significant excess mortality in older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female named Grace with mild cognitive impairment lives alone in a rural area. She has no family nearby, rarely leaves the home, has not seen a friend in 3 months, and scores positively on the UCLA Loneliness Scale. She primary care clinician is concerned about her safety and well-being. Which of the following represents the most comprehensive clinical approach to address social isolation in this older adult?', '["Prescribe a high-dose benzodiazepine to reduce the patient''s anxiety associated with social isolation", "Discharge the patient from geriatric care as social isolation is a personal lifestyle choice outside the scope of medical practice", "Recommend the patient immediately move to a large urban area where social opportunities are more available", "Advise the patient to join a social media platform, as this is the primary evidence-based intervention for social isolation in older adults", "Screen formally for loneliness and social isolation; connect with the Area Agency on Aging for services (Meals on Wheels, telephone reassurance, friendly visitor programs, senior center transportation); evaluate for depression; and consider telehealth check-ins to maintain medical oversight"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Social isolation and loneliness** are major social determinants of health in older adults, associated with a 26% increased risk of mortality, cognitive decline, depression, and reduced physical activity. Community-dwelling older adults living alone in rural areas face compounded risk. Evidence-based interventions include: (1) **Meals on Wheels** — provides nutrition AND a daily welfare check; (2) **Telephone reassurance programs** — regular calls from trained volunteers; (3) **Friendly visitor programs** — in-person visits; (4) **Senior center programs** — group activities, transportation assistance; (5) **Telehealth** — video-based medical visits maintain connection with the healthcare team; (6) **Depression screening** (PHQ-9) — loneliness and depression are bidirectional; (7) **Caregiver and community health worker** involvement. The **Area Agency on Aging (AAA)** is the primary local gateway for all these services. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
