ess and depression are bidirectional; (7) **Caregiver and community health worker** involvement. The **Area Agency on Aging (AAA)** is the primary local gateway for all these services. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female named Elizabeth is brought to the emergency department by police after being found confused and sleeping in a park. She has a history of alcohol use disorder, hypertension, and is found to be in early-moderate cognitive decline on bedside testing. She states she has been without stable housing for 3 months. Which of the following represents the most comprehensive approach to this patient''s care?', '["Discharge the patient from the emergency department with a bus token and a list of local churches, as housing is not a medical issue", "Prescribe naltrexone for alcohol use disorder and arrange a clinic follow-up appointment only, without addressing the patient''s housing crisis", "Medically stabilize the patient; arrange a social work consultation for Housing First program referral, substance use disorder treatment, Medicaid enrollment, and connection to supportive housing with case management services; perform a formal cognitive and capacity assessment", "Advise the patient to apply for Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) and wait 2 years for Medicare eligibility before receiving any further care", "Admit the patient to a locked psychiatric facility for indefinite care due to cognitive impairment combined with homelessness"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Older adult homelessness** (the ''graying of homelessness'') is a growing public health crisis. Older homeless individuals have a median age of death of ~51 years and have dramatically higher rates of cognitive impairment, falls, hypothermia, infectious disease, and hospitalizations. **Housing First** is the evidence-based model that provides immediate, permanent housing without sobriety prerequisites, then layers in medical and social services. Key interventions: (1) **Housing First program referral** — immediate permanent supportive housing; (2) **Medicaid enrollment** — for uninsured homeless older adults; (3) **Medical stabilization** — address all acute and chronic conditions; (4) **Cognitive assessment** — determine capacity and guardianship needs; (5) **Substance use disorder treatment** — integrated within housing support; (6) **Case management** — multidisciplinary coordination. Homelessness is a medical diagnosis context requiring active physician engagement. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
