    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male named Arthur is brought to the emergency department by police after being found confused and sleeping in a park. He has a history of alcohol use disorder, hypertension, and is found to be in early-moderate cognitive decline on bedside testing. He states he has been without stable housing for 4 months. Which of the following represents the most comprehensive approach to this patient''s care?', '["Admit the patient to a locked psychiatric facility for indefinite care due to cognitive impairment combined with homelessness", "Prescribe naltrexone for alcohol use disorder and arrange a clinic follow-up appointment only, without addressing the patient''s housing crisis", "Advise the patient to apply for Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) and wait 2 years for Medicare eligibility before receiving any further care", "Discharge the patient from the emergency department with a bus token and a list of local churches, as housing is not a medical issue", "Medically stabilize the patient; arrange a social work consultation for Housing First program referral, substance use disorder treatment, Medicaid enrollment, and connection to supportive housing with case management services; perform a formal cognitive and capacity assessment"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Older adult homelessness** (the ''graying of homelessness'') is a growing public health crisis. Older homeless individuals have a median age of death of ~51 years and have dramatically higher rates of cognitive impairment, falls, hypothermia, infectious disease, and hospitalizations. **Housing First** is the evidence-based model that provides immediate, permanent housing without sobriety prerequisites, then layers in medical and social services. Key interventions: (1) **Housing First program referral** — immediate permanent supportive housing; (2) **Medicaid enrollment** — for uninsured homeless older adults; (3) **Medical stabilization** — address all acute and chronic conditions; (4) **Cognitive assessment** — determine capacity and guardianship needs; (5) **Substance use disorder treatment** — integrated within housing support; (6) **Case management** — multidisciplinary coordination. Homelessness is a medical diagnosis context requiring active physician engagement. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In Texas, a public health official named Lois is presenting a community initiative to a group of geriatricians. The initiative targets a large urban apartment complex where over 60% of residents are adults ≥ 65 years old who have ''aged in place'' in their original apartments over several decades. Many residents have multiple chronic conditions, functional impairments, and limited social support networks, but strongly prefer to remain in their homes. Which type of community-based intervention model is most specifically designed for this type of naturally occurring aging community?', '["A Medicare Annual Wellness Visit campaign, which provides a single annual assessment but no ongoing community-based services", "A Naturally Occurring Retirement Community (NORC) Supportive Service Program (NORC-SSP), which brings coordinated health, social, and supportive services directly into residential buildings where a large proportion of residents are older adults", "The State Children''s Health Insurance Program (SCHIP), which is exclusively designed for pediatric populations and has no relevance to older adult housing", "A hospital-based acute care for elders (ACE) unit, which is limited to inpatient hospitalized patients and is not applicable to community settings", "A state-mandated mass relocation program to move all residents with ADL limitations to skilled nursing facilities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A **Naturally Occurring Retirement Community (NORC)** is a residential community (apartment building, neighborhood) NOT originally designed for older adults but in which a large proportion of residents (≥ 40–60%) are ≥ 60 years old because they have ''aged in place.'' NORC-based **Supportive Service Programs (NORC-SSPs)** bring coordinated services INTO the existing residential community: health screenings, case management, social programming, fitness classes, transportation assistance, and links to community agencies — without requiring residents to move. This model preserves **aging in place** while overcoming service access barriers. The **Village model** is a related peer-driven community organization that helps older adults age in place through volunteer services and community connection. Both models address housing-related social determinants without requiring institutionalization. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In Florida, a public health official named Kenneth is presenting a community initiative to a group of geriatricians. The initiative targets a large urban apartment complex where over 60% of residents are adults ≥ 65 years old who have ''aged in place'' in their original apartments over several decades. Many residents have multiple chronic conditions, functional impairments, and limited social support networks, but strongly prefer to remain in their homes. Which type of community-based intervention model is most specifically designed for this type of naturally occurring aging community?', '["A Naturally Occurring Retirement Community (NORC) Supportive Service Program (NORC-SSP), which brings coordinated health, social, and supportive services directly into residential buildings where a large proportion of residents are older adults", "A state-mandated mass relocation program to move all residents with ADL limitations to skilled nursing facilities", "A Medicare Annual Wellness Visit campaign, which provides a single annual assessment but no ongoing community-based services", "A hospital-based acute care for elders (ACE) unit, which is limited to inpatient hospitalized patients and is not applicable to community settings", "The State Children''s Health Insurance Program (SCHIP), which is exclusively designed for pediatric populations and has no relevance to older adult housing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A **Naturally Occurring Retirement Community (NORC)** is a residential community (apartment building, neighborhood) NOT originally designed for older adults but in which a large proportion of residents (≥ 40–60%) are ≥ 60 years old because they have ''aged in place.'' NORC-based **Supportive Service Programs (NORC-SSPs)** bring coordinated services INTO the existing residential community: health screenings, case management, social programming, fitness classes, transportation assistance, and links to community agencies — without requiring residents to move. This model preserves **aging in place** while overcoming service access barriers. The **Village model** is a related peer-driven community organization that helps older adults age in place through volunteer services and community connection. Both models address housing-related social determinants without requiring institutionalization. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
