del preserves **aging in place** while overcoming service access barriers. The **Village model** is a related peer-driven community organization that helps older adults age in place through volunteer services and community connection. Both models address housing-related social determinants without requiring institutionalization. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male named Bernard with moderate osteoarthritis and mild balance impairment is planning to remodel his home to enable safe aging in place for the next decade. His occupational therapist recommends incorporating ''universal design'' principles. Which of the following best describes the concept of universal design in the context of aging-in-place home modifications?', '["Universal design refers exclusively to the installation of overhead patient-lift systems (Hoyer lifts) in every room of the home", "Universal design requires replacing all existing home appliances with hospital-grade medical equipment to create a clinical care environment at home", "Universal design refers to the design of environments and products that are usable by all people to the greatest extent possible without adaptation, including wider doorways for wheelchairs, zero-step entries, lever-style door handles, curbless showers, and varied height countertops", "Universal design requires that the home be rebuilt from the foundation using only government-approved building materials rated for geriatric occupancy", "Universal design means that all home modifications must be pre-approved by Medicare before they can be considered medically legitimate"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Universal design** is an architectural philosophy that creates spaces usable by people of all ages and abilities without the need for specialized adaptation. Key principles for aging-in-place: (1) **Zero-step entries** (no threshold barriers, no front-step curbs); (2) **Wider doorways** (≥32–36 inches) to accommodate mobility aids; (3) **Lever-style door handles and faucets** (easier with arthritis/limited grip); (4) **Single-story living** (bedroom, bathroom, kitchen on one floor); (5) **Curbless/roll-in shower** (eliminates tub transfer risk); (6) **Varied-height counters and adjustable shelving**; (7) **Adequate lighting and contrast** (light switches at reachable heights). The **CAPABLE program** (OT + nurse + home repair) and **Area Agency on Aging** home modification grants help fund these changes for low-income older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 41; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old male named Arthur with moderate osteoarthritis and mild balance impairment is planning to remodel his home to enable safe aging in place for the next decade. His occupational therapist recommends incorporating ''universal design'' principles. Which of the following best describes the concept of universal design in the context of aging-in-place home modifications?', '["Universal design requires replacing all existing home appliances with hospital-grade medical equipment to create a clinical care environment at home", "Universal design means that all home modifications must be pre-approved by Medicare before they can be considered medically legitimate", "Universal design refers to the design of environments and products that are usable by all people to the greatest extent possible without adaptation, including wider doorways for wheelchairs, zero-step entries, lever-style door handles, curbless showers, and varied height countertops", "Universal design requires that the home be rebuilt from the foundation using only government-approved building materials rated for geriatric occupancy", "Universal design refers exclusively to the installation of overhead patient-lift systems (Hoyer lifts) in every room of the home"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Universal design** is an architectural philosophy that creates spaces usable by people of all ages and abilities without the need for specialized adaptation. Key principles for aging-in-place: (1) **Zero-step entries** (no threshold barriers, no front-step curbs); (2) **Wider doorways** (≥32–36 inches) to accommodate mobility aids; (3) **Lever-style door handles and faucets** (easier with arthritis/limited grip); (4) **Single-story living** (bedroom, bathroom, kitchen on one floor); (5) **Curbless/roll-in shower** (eliminates tub transfer risk); (6) **Varied-height counters and adjustable shelving**; (7) **Adequate lighting and contrast** (light switches at reachable heights). The **CAPABLE program** (OT + nurs