se + home repair) and **Area Agency on Aging** home modification grants help fund these changes for low-income older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 41; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female named Irene with moderate osteoarthritis and mild balance impairment is planning to remodel her home to enable safe aging in place for the next decade. Her occupational therapist recommends incorporating ''universal design'' principles. Which of the following best describes the concept of universal design in the context of aging-in-place home modifications?', '["Universal design requires that the home be rebuilt from the foundation using only government-approved building materials rated for geriatric occupancy", "Universal design means that all home modifications must be pre-approved by Medicare before they can be considered medically legitimate", "Universal design refers to the design of environments and products that are usable by all people to the greatest extent possible without adaptation, including wider doorways for wheelchairs, zero-step entries, lever-style door handles, curbless showers, and varied height countertops", "Universal design refers exclusively to the installation of overhead patient-lift systems (Hoyer lifts) in every room of the home", "Universal design requires replacing all existing home appliances with hospital-grade medical equipment to create a clinical care environment at home"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Universal design** is an architectural philosophy that creates spaces usable by people of all ages and abilities without the need for specialized adaptation. Key principles for aging-in-place: (1) **Zero-step entries** (no threshold barriers, no front-step curbs); (2) **Wider doorways** (≥32–36 inches) to accommodate mobility aids; (3) **Lever-style door handles and faucets** (easier with arthritis/limited grip); (4) **Single-story living** (bedroom, bathroom, kitchen on one floor); (5) **Curbless/roll-in shower** (eliminates tub transfer risk); (6) **Varied-height counters and adjustable shelving**; (7) **Adequate lighting and contrast** (light switches at reachable heights). The **CAPABLE program** (OT + nurse + home repair) and **Area Agency on Aging** home modification grants help fund these changes for low-income older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 41; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Gladys with moderate osteoarthritis and mild balance impairment is planning to remodel her home to enable safe aging in place for the next decade. Her occupational therapist recommends incorporating ''universal design'' principles. Which of the following best describes the concept of universal design in the context of aging-in-place home modifications?', '["Universal design requires replacing all existing home appliances with hospital-grade medical equipment to create a clinical care environment at home", "Universal design refers exclusively to the installation of overhead patient-lift systems (Hoyer lifts) in every room of the home", "Universal design requires that the home be rebuilt from the foundation using only government-approved building materials rated for geriatric occupancy", "Universal design refers to the design of environments and products that are usable by all people to the greatest extent possible without adaptation, including wider doorways for wheelchairs, zero-step entries, lever-style door handles, curbless showers, and varied height countertops", "Universal design means that all home modifications must be pre-approved by Medicare before they can be considered medically legitimate"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Universal design** is an architectural philosophy that creates spaces usable by people of all ages and abilities without the need for specialized adaptation. Key principles for aging-in-place: (1) **Zero-step entries** (no threshold barriers, no front-step curbs); (2) **Wider doorways** (≥32–36 inches) to accommodate mobility aids; (3) **Lever-style door handles and faucets** (easier with arthritis/limited grip); (4) **Single-story living** (bedroom, bathroom, kitchen on one floor); (5) **Curbless/roll-in shower** (eliminates tub transfer risk); (6) **Varied-height counters and adjustable shelving**; (7) **Adequate lighting and contrast** (light switches at reachable heights). The **CAPABLE program** (OT + nurse + home repair) and **Area Agency on Aging** home modification grants help fund these changes for low-income older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 41; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female named Martha lives in a rundown studio apartment and pays 80% of her $720/month fixed income in rent, leaving insufficient money for food and medications. She is functionally independent with no ADL limitations. Her clinician wants to explore affordable, safe housing alternatives. Which of the following federal housing programs is specifically designed to provide subsidized, supportive housing for very low-income older adults who are functionally independent?', '["Medicaid''s Institutional Care Program, which provides housing only in certified nursing facilities and is not applicable to independent community-dwelling older adults", "The Veterans Affairs Aid and Attendance Benefit, which is exclusively available to eligible veterans and their spouses and is not applicable to the general older adult population", "The Section 8 Housing Choice Voucher Program, which exclusively serves families with children under 18 years old and explicitly excludes older adult applicants", "The Medicare Part A institutional care benefit, which covers permanent residential living in skilled nursing facilities for all low-income Medicare beneficiaries", "HUD Section 202 Supportive Housing for the Elderly Program, which provides federally subsidized housing units with rents capped at 30% of income, along with access to supportive services coordination for functionally independent low-income older adults"]'::jsonb, 4, '**HUD Section 202 Supportive Housing for the Elderly** is a federal program providing capital financing to develop affordable housing for very low-income older adults (≥62 years) who are functionally independent or nearly so. Key features: (1) Rent is capped at **30% of adjusted gross income** (standard affordable housing benchmark); (2) Units are **service-enhanced** — a service coordinator helps residents access health, social, and community services; (3) Residents maintain their own apartments (independent living, not assisted living); (4) Waiting lists are often very long (months to years). Other relevant programs: **Section 8 Housing Choice Vouchers** (tenant-based rental assistance, not limited to children), **Low Income Housing Tax Credit (LIHTC)** developments. The 30% income benchmark for affordable housing is a key concept. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
