    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old male named Fred lives in a rundown studio apartment and pays 80% of his $720/month fixed income in rent, leaving insufficient money for food and medications. He is functionally independent with no ADL limitations. His clinician wants to explore affordable, safe housing alternatives. Which of the following federal housing programs is specifically designed to provide subsidized, supportive housing for very low-income older adults who are functionally independent?', '["Medicaid''s Institutional Care Program, which provides housing only in certified nursing facilities and is not applicable to independent community-dwelling older adults", "HUD Section 202 Supportive Housing for the Elderly Program, which provides federally subsidized housing units with rents capped at 30% of income, along with access to supportive services coordination for functionally independent low-income older adults", "The Veterans Affairs Aid and Attendance Benefit, which is exclusively available to eligible veterans and their spouses and is not applicable to the general older adult population", "The Medicare Part A institutional care benefit, which covers permanent residential living in skilled nursing facilities for all low-income Medicare beneficiaries", "The Section 8 Housing Choice Voucher Program, which exclusively serves families with children under 18 years old and explicitly excludes older adult applicants"]'::jsonb, 1, '**HUD Section 202 Supportive Housing for the Elderly** is a federal program providing capital financing to develop affordable housing for very low-income older adults (≥62 years) who are functionally independent or nearly so. Key features: (1) Rent is capped at **30% of adjusted gross income** (standard affordable housing benchmark); (2) Units are **service-enhanced** — a service coordinator helps residents access health, social, and community services; (3) Residents maintain their own apartments (independent living, not assisted living); (4) Waiting lists are often very long (months to years). Other relevant programs: **Section 8 Housing Choice Vouchers** (tenant-based rental assistance, not limited to children), **Low Income Housing Tax Credit (LIHTC)** developments. The 30% income benchmark for affordable housing is a key concept. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A social worker reports to a geriatrician that a 79-year-old female named Alice with mild dementia is living in a small, unlicensed boarding home with 5 other older adults. The home is run by a private individual who charges monthly fees for room, board, and minimal assistance. There are no licensed nursing staff, no medication management policies, and no oversight by a state regulatory agency. What is the primary clinical and ethical concern in this situation?', '["Unlicensed board-and-care homes exist in a significant regulatory gap; residents are at high risk for neglect, financial exploitation, substandard care, and safety hazards; the clinician should facilitate a formal home safety and welfare assessment, report concerns to Adult Protective Services (APS), and explore licensed residential alternatives", "Unlicensed facilities are not subject to any reporting obligations, and clinicians should not involve themselves in patients'' private housing arrangements", "The patient should be immediately arrested for illegal habitation in an unlicensed facility", "This arrangement is optimal because the patient is receiving personalized care in a small-group home setting and should be encouraged to sign a long-term lease immediately", "The home operator should be immediately licensed retroactively as a nurse practitioner to legitimize the care provided"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Board-and-care homes** (also called ''adult residential care facilities,'' ''personal care homes,'' or ''adult foster homes'') are small group residential settings that provide room, board, and basic custodial assistance. **Unlicensed** facilities are common and represent a significant **regulatory gap**: they are not subject to the federal standards that govern licensed assisted living or nursing homes (OBRA 1987). Risks include: (1) **Neglect** (inadequate staffing, unsafe medications management); (2) **Financial exploitation** (opaque fee structures); (3) **Physical safety hazards** (fire, fall risks); (4) **No medical oversight**. The clinician''s obligation: (1) conduct or facilitate a welfare check; (2) report to **Adult Protective Services (APS)** if neglect is suspected; (3) explore licensed residential care options. This is a key housing-related social determinant of health issue in geriatric practice. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A social worker reports to a geriatrician that a 73-year-old female named Florence with mild dementia is living in a small, unlicensed boarding home with 5 other older adults. The home is run by a private individual who charges monthly fees for room, board, and minimal assistance. There are no licensed nursing staff, no medication management policies, and no oversight by a state regulatory agency. What is the primary clinical and ethical concern in this situation?', '["This arrangement is optimal because the patient is receiving personalized care in a small-group home setting and should be encouraged to sign a long-term lease immediately", "Unlicensed board-and-care homes exist in a significant regulatory gap; residents are at high risk for neglect, financial exploitation, substandard care, and safety hazards; the clinician should facilitate a formal home safety and welfare assessment, report concerns to Adult Protective Services (APS), and explore licensed residential alternatives", "The home operator should be immediately licensed retroactively as a nurse practitioner to legitimize the care provided", "The patient should be immediately arrested for illegal habitation in an unlicensed facility", "Unlicensed facilities are not subject to any reporting obligations, and clinicians should not involve themselves in patients'' private housing arrangements"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Board-and-care homes** (also called ''adult residential care facilities,'' ''personal care homes,'' or ''adult foster homes'') are small group residential settings that provide room, board, and basic custodial assistance. **Unlicensed** facilities are common and represent a significant **regulatory gap**: they are not subject to the federal standards that govern licensed assisted living or nursing homes (OBRA 1987). Risks include: (1) **Neglect** (inadequate staffing, unsafe medications management); (2) **Financial exploitation** (opaque fee structures); (3) **Physical safety hazards** (fire, fall risks); (4) **No medical oversight**. The clinician''s obligation: (1) conduct or facilitate a welfare check; (2) report to **Adult Protective Services (APS)** if neglect is suspected; (3) explore licensed residential care options. This is a key housing-related social determinant of health issue in geriatric practice. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A social worker reports to a geriatrician that a 93-year-old female named Bernice with mild dementia is living in a small, unlicensed boarding home with 5 other older adults. The home is run by a private individual who charges monthly fees for room, board, and minimal assistance. There are no licensed nursing staff, no medication management policies, and no oversight by a state regulatory agency. What is the primary clinical and ethical concern in this situation?', '["The patient should be immediately arrested for illegal habitation in an unlicensed facility", "The home operator should be immediately licensed retroactively as a nurse practitioner to legitimize the care provided", "Unlicensed facilities are not subject to any reporting obligations, and clinicians should not involve themselves in patients'' private housing arrangements", "This arrangement is optimal because the patient is receiving personalized care in a small-group home setting and should be encouraged to sign a long-term lease immediately", "Unlicensed board-and-care homes exist in a significant regulatory gap; residents are at high risk for neglect, financial exploitation, substandard care, and safety hazards; the clinician should facilitate a formal home safety and welfare assessment, report concerns to Adult Protective Services (APS), and explore licensed residential alternatives"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Board-and-care homes** (also called ''adult residential care facilities,'' ''personal care homes,'' or ''adult foster homes'') are small group residential settings that provide room, board, and basic custodial assistance. **Unlicensed** facilities are common and represent a significant **regulatory gap**: they are not subject to the federal standards that govern licensed assisted living or nursing homes (OBRA 1987). Risks include: (1) **Neglect** (inadequate staffing, unsafe medications management); (2) **Financial exploitation** (opaque fee structures); (3) **Physical safety hazards** (fire, fall risks); (4) **No medical oversight**. The clinician''s obligation: (1) conduct or facilitate a welfare check; (2) report to **Adult Protective Services (APS)** if neglect is suspected; (3) explore licensed residential care options. This is a key housing-related social determinant of health issue in geriatric practice. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
